# The Worker's secrets, in dotenv format.
#
# TWO READERS, and the first one dictates the shape of this file:
#
#   • The Deploy to Cloudflare button builds its setup dialog from this file.
#     Measured on a real press, 2026-09-07 — not from the docs, which say none of
#     it:
#       – every UNCOMMENTED key here becomes a field in the dialog;
#       – every field is REQUIRED. The browser refuses to submit an empty one
#         ("Please fill out this field."), so there is no such thing as an
#         optional prompt;
#       – the VALUE here becomes that field's prefilled default. This file once
#         said `change-me`, which offered every presser a CDN whose session
#         cookie was signed with a string printed in a public repo;
#       – the `description`s in package.json `cloudflare.bindings` DO NOT RENDER
#         for secrets (they do for the R2 bucket). Guidance written there is
#         invisible at the moment it is needed.
#
#     Between those, one rule: a key belongs here ONLY if a first deploy cannot
#     work without it. Anything else would be a required box with no explanation
#     that a stranger must put something in — and whatever they type becomes a
#     real credential on a real Worker.
#
#   • Local dev — `cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars`, uncomment what you need, and
#     `wrangler dev` picks it up. .dev.vars is gitignored, and `bun run types`
#     reads it to type env.CDN_* in the generated Env.
#
# So exactly one key is uncommented. The other three are documented below and
# set, when you want them, with `wrangler secret put <NAME>` — or by `cdn setup`,
# which generates two of them for you. Until they exist the paths they guard are
# CLOSED, never open, which is why leaving them out is safe.

# REQUIRED — and the only thing the deploy dialog asks you for.
#
# The explorer's login. It is a password field, so let your password manager
# generate it; it also signs the session cookie unless CDN_SESSION_SECRET is set,
# which is the other reason to make it a good one. With no password at all the
# explorer answers 503 and says so: object URLs keep working, nobody can sign in.
CDN_PASSWORD=

# ── the rest: not prompted for, not required, safe to never set ───────────────

# Bearer for POST /api/upload — the write path used by scripts, the CLI, the
# Claude Code hooks and the macOS Shortcut. Unset means every bearer is rejected,
# so a fresh CDN has no machine write path rather than a guessable one; you can
# still upload by dragging onto the explorer. `cdn setup` generates one, sets it,
# and stores it at ~/.config/cdn/hosts/<host>.env so the CLI works immediately.
#
#   CDN_UPLOAD_TOKEN=

# Signs the explorer's session cookie. Unset derives the key from CDN_PASSWORD,
# which is what makes the one-field deploy above possible. Set it to decouple the
# two — rotating sessions without changing your password, or the reverse:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   CDN_SESSION_SECRET=

# A Cloudflare API token scoped to Zone → Cache Purge on your zone and nothing
# else. With it (plus the CDN_ZONE_ID / CDN_PUBLIC_ORIGIN vars in wrangler.jsonc)
# a write invalidates every edge POP, so an overwrite is visible immediately
# worldwide. Unset means the purge is local-POP only — the fallback, not an
# error. `cdn setup` mints this one for you, correctly scoped.
#
#   CDN_PURGE_TOKEN=
